slate: 'internal error: unknown typedef' spanning the whole definition. It is an Internal error, so the checker let something through that lowering can't resolve. The typedef name in the returned function type's parameter list is probably not bound in names.rs for definitions, since only the outer parameter list is treated as the function's prototype scope. The same shape appears in signal()-style definitions.

Acceptance: fixture in clang/linux/x86_64, and pngstest.c passes (with zlib include).

  typedef float __m128 __attribute__((__vector_size__(16), __aligned__(16)));
  __m128 g;

clang accepts on any target; slate fails with "unsupported in numeric IR lowering: target builtin type" on i686/x86_64 windows-msvc and on i686-linux-gnu. Renaming the typedef (vf4) makes it pass, so the name __m128 resolves to slate's own target builtin type instead of the user's typedef. Not MS-specific; found by the MS sweep.

